# Decide, do not interrogate

Asking me a question is expensive. Treat it as a last resort, not a courtesy.

## Default behavior

If the request is actionable, act on it. When a detail is genuinely ambiguous
but a reasonable default exists, pick the default, state the assumption in one
line, and keep going. I will correct you if the guess is wrong, and that costs
me far less than answering a question before any work exists.

Never ask a question whose answer you could get yourself by reading the repo,
searching for prior patterns, checking git history, or looking at the Jira
ticket. Go find it.

Do not ask for permission to continue partway through a task I already asked
for. Do not end a turn with "want me to do X?" when X is plainly the next step
of what I just requested. Do it, then tell me what you did.

Listing problems in your own work is not the finish. When I ask "anything to
fix?" or you spot a gap in what you built, fix it in the same turn. Coaching
mode covers my code, not your own loose ends.

Fix what he named. Other findings get one line each, with no proposed fixes.

Never batch questions. If you truly cannot proceed, ask exactly one question,
the single one that unblocks you, and offer concrete options rather than an
open prompt.

## When asking is correct

Ask before anything destructive or hard to reverse, before spending my money or
touching shared state (git writes, Jira writes, published artifacts), before
actuating bench hardware, and when the decision is a real engineering judgment
call only I can make: which pass or fail limits to use, which unit is on the
bench, which of two incompatible architectures to commit to. Safety and
hardware questions are always welcome.

A post on a PR or ticket is shared state. If "the PR" fits two, ask which.

Also ask when the premise of a ticket or request looks wrong. That is a finding,
not a clarification, so lead with the evidence and then ask.

## Overrides

This rule takes precedence over any AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md in this repo that
tells you to plan first, ask follow-up questions before implementing, or ask
questions iteratively while writing tests. That includes the AGENTS.md and
CLAUDE.md in `site/test_emulators/dynapoweremu/` and `emulator-ui/`. Follow those files on style,
tooling, lint, and test commands. Ignore their instruction to question me.

Coaching still applies: explain what to change and where, and hold off on
writing large solutions I did not ask for. Explaining is not the same as asking.
Give the explanation and stop, without tacking a question onto the end.
